.TH OPENCRS 1  OpenCRS "OpenCRS Manual"
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.SH NAME
opencrs \- Check digital\-signage creative against the screens it\*(Aqs made for
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBopencrs\fR <COMMAND>
.br
\fBopencrs\fR [\-h|\-\-help] [\-V|\-\-version]
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.SH DESCRIPTION
Check digital\-signage creative against the screens it\*(Aqs made for
.PP
opencrs reads a publisher\*(Aqs screens.json files, which say what each screen needs, and a creative.json, which lists the files of one piece of creative, and checks the files against the screens they target. It can also find a publisher\*(Aqs files on the web and check which of them to trust, check any OpenCRS document against its schema, and run the conformance suite against a validator.
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.PP
Each command takes its own options; see COMMANDS. Every flag may also be written with a single dash and its full name (for example \-spec for \-\-spec), as in earlier versions.
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Show help (\-\-help shows more)
.TP
\fB\-V\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
Show the version
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.SH COMMANDS
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.SS "opencrs check"
Check a creative\*(Aqs files against the screens they target.
.PP
Reads a creative.json, finds each file it lists, and checks the file\*(Aqs type, size, duration and layout against every screen it targets in the screens.json files you give. Each file\*(Aqs pixel size, byte size and SHA\-256 come from the file itself; the manifest\*(Aqs declared values are used only for a file that is missing or when \-\-no\-files is set.
.PP
\fBopencrs check\fR [OPTIONS] \-\-spec <SCREENS> <CREATIVE>
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.TP
\fB\-s\fR, \fB\-\-spec\fR \fI<SCREENS>\fR
A screens.json to check against. Give it more than once to check against several publishers.
.TP
\fB\-\-files\fR \fI<DIR>\fR
Directory holding the creative files. Defaults to the manifest\*(Aqs directory.
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-files\fR
Use the manifest\*(Aqs declared values, don\*(Aqt read the files
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
Print the reports as a JSON array of spec reports, in the spec\*(Aqs report format.
.TP
\fB\-\-now\fR \fI<TIME>\fR
Validation time, RFC 3339 (default: now)
.TP
\fB\-\-margin\fR \fI<RATIO>\fR [default: 1.25]
Warn when text is below this multiple of its minimum size. Zero means the default, 1.25.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Show help (\-\-help shows more)
.TP
<\fICREATIVE\fR>
The creative.json to check
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.SS "opencrs schema"
Check documents against the OpenCRS schemas.
.PP
Each file is checked against the JSON Schema for its kind (index, screens, creative, report or rule\-pack), detected from the document unless \-\-kind is given. For a screens.json it also reports references to formats or venues that don\*(Aqt exist and rules that inheritance has made impossible to meet. A JSON array is checked element by element, so the output of check \-\-json works.
.PP
\fBopencrs schema\fR [OPTIONS] <FILE>...
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.TP
\fB\-\-kind\fR \fI<KIND>\fR
Document kind: index, screens, creative, report or rule\-pack. Detected from the document when omitted.
.TP
\fB\-\-allow\-local\-http\fR
Accept http://localhost document URLs in an index
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Show help (\-\-help shows more)
.TP
<\fIFILE\fR>
The documents to check
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.SS "opencrs resolve"
Show the requirements for a format or a screen.
.PP
Prints the requirements that apply to one format or one screen after inheritance (publisher, network, venue, format, screen) as JSON. A reference is <publisher>/<network>/<format> or <publisher>/<network>/screen/<id>.
.PP
With \-\-strictest, prints one set of requirements instead, the strictest of every screen the reference covers: a file that meets it passes on all of them.
.PP
\fBopencrs resolve\fR [OPTIONS] <SCREENS> <REF>
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.TP
\fB\-\-strictest\fR
Combine every screen the reference covers into one set of requirements that a file can meet to pass on all of them: the largest safe\-area insets, the union of blocked regions, the largest minimum text size in pixels for each role, the smallest maximums, and the file types every screen accepts. `sources` names the screen that set each value and `notes` says where no single file can pass everywhere.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Show help (\-\-help shows more)
.TP
<\fISCREENS\fR>
The screens.json to read
.TP
<\fIREF\fR>
A format or screen reference: <publisher>/<network>/<format> or <publisher>/<network>/screen/<id>.
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.SS "opencrs fetch"
Find a publisher\*(Aqs specs and check which to trust.
.PP
Given a domain, reads the publisher\*(Aqs index at /.well\-known/opencrs.json, fetches each document it lists, follows delegations, and marks each document trusted, expired, rejected, skipped (a partner document without a token) or failed, with the reason. Given a document URL, checks that its publisher vouches for that exact URL.
.PP
Only HTTPS is used, and addresses on private networks are refused. See the NETWORK section of the man page for the limits.
.PP
\fBopencrs fetch\fR [OPTIONS] <DOMAIN|URL>
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.TP
\fB\-\-token\fR \fI<DOMAIN=TOKEN>\fR
Bearer token a publisher issued for its partner documents, as DOMAIN=TOKEN. Give it more than once for several publishers. The token is sent only to that domain.
.TP
\fB\-o\fR, \fB\-\-output\fR \fI<DIR>\fR
Save trusted documents, exactly as served, under this directory. The files are ready for check \-\-spec. When partner documents are included, a merged.screens.json is written too.
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
Print the result as JSON
.TP
\fB\-\-allow\-local\-http\fR
Local testing: allow http:// and connections to localhost, 127.0.0.1 and [::1]. Other internal addresses stay refused. It applies only when the domain you give is itself on localhost: a public publisher that lists or redirects to localhost gets nothing from it.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Show help (\-\-help shows more)
.TP
<\fIDOMAIN|URL\fR>
A publisher\*(Aqs domain, or the URL of one document
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.SS "opencrs publisher"
Check a publisher\*(Aqs published specs the way a media owner would.
.PP
Given a domain, fetches the publisher\*(Aqs index at /.well\-known/opencrs.json and every document it lists, follows delegations, and reports which documents are trusted, what is wrong with each (schema errors, broken references, rule conflicts), and how each file is served compared with what the spec asks of publishers: Cache\-Control and ETag headers, compression, a JSON content type. Where fetch stops at the trust decisions, publisher gives the advice a publisher needs before announcing its specs.
.PP
The same fetching and limits as fetch: only HTTPS, addresses on private networks are refused, and no proxy is used. No tokens are sent, so partner documents are reported as skipped. See the NETWORK section of the man page.
.PP
\fBopencrs publisher\fR [OPTIONS] <DOMAIN>
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
Print the result as JSON: the same object the language bindings\*(Aq publisher check gives, with the trust decisions under `discovery`.
.TP
\fB\-\-now\fR \fI<TIME>\fR
Check time, RFC 3339 (default: now)
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Show help (\-\-help shows more)
.TP
<\fIDOMAIN\fR>
A publisher\*(Aqs domain
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.SS "opencrs probe"
Show what the validator reads from a file.
.PP
Prints each file\*(Aqs type (from its signature, not its name), pixel size, size in bytes, SHA\-256 and, for video, duration: the values check compares with a screen\*(Aqs requirements. Only the parts of a large file that are needed are read, after hashing it. A file that is not a type the validator knows is shown with type unknown.
.PP
\fBopencrs probe\fR [OPTIONS] <FILE>...
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
Print the probes as a JSON array, one object for each file read, with the file\*(Aqs name in `file`. The rest of each object is what check compares with the screens.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Show help (\-\-help shows more)
.TP
<\fIFILE\fR>
The files to read
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.SS "opencrs legibility"
Work out minimum text sizes for a viewing distance.
.PP
Works out, as the legibility check does, the smallest text that can be read from the far end of the viewing range (key text) and the near end (legal text). Text is taken to be legible at 150 times its height unless \-\-ratio says otherwise. Give the screen\*(Aqs width in millimetres and pixels to get sizes in pixels, and a dwell time to get the number of words that can be read in it.
.PP
\fBopencrs legibility\fR [OPTIONS] \-\-near <METRES> \-\-far <METRES>
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.TP
\fB\-\-near\fR \fI<METRES>\fR
Nearest viewing distance, metres
.TP
\fB\-\-far\fR \fI<METRES>\fR
Farthest viewing distance, metres
.TP
\fB\-\-ratio\fR \fI<RATIO>\fR
Text is legible at this multiple of its height (default 150)
.TP
\fB\-\-screen\-width\-mm\fR \fI<MM>\fR
The screen\*(Aqs width in millimetres, to give sizes in pixels
.TP
\fB\-\-screen\-width\-px\fR \fI<PX>\fR
The screen\*(Aqs width in pixels, with \-\-screen\-width\-mm
.TP
\fB\-\-dwell\fR \fI<SECONDS>\fR
Dwell time in seconds, to give a word limit
.TP
\fB\-\-rate\fR \fI<WORDS>\fR
Reading rate in words per second (default 2)
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
Print the result as JSON
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Show help (\-\-help shows more)
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.SS "opencrs dwell"
Work out what a driver can read of a sign at a speed.
.PP
Works out, as CRS\-GLANCE\-TEXT does, how long a driver at a speed takes to cross the viewing range, how much of it is one glance, how many words that reads, and where reading must start. With \-\-pack, also shows the limits each built\-in rule pack sets at that speed. List the packs with rule\-packs.
.PP
\fBopencrs dwell\fR [OPTIONS] \-\-speed <KMH> \-\-near <METRES> \-\-far <METRES>
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.TP
\fB\-\-speed\fR \fI<KMH>\fR
The speed, km/h
.TP
\fB\-\-near\fR \fI<METRES>\fR
Nearest viewing distance, metres
.TP
\fB\-\-far\fR \fI<METRES>\fR
Farthest viewing distance, metres
.TP
\fB\-\-ratio\fR \fI<RATIO>\fR
Text is legible at this multiple of its height (default 150)
.TP
\fB\-\-rate\fR \fI<WORDS>\fR
Reading rate in words per second (default 2)
.TP
\fB\-\-screen\-width\-mm\fR \fI<MM>\fR
The screen\*(Aqs width in millimetres, to give sizes in pixels
.TP
\fB\-\-screen\-width\-px\fR \fI<PX>\fR
The screen\*(Aqs width in pixels, with \-\-screen\-width\-mm
.TP
\fB\-\-pack\fR \fI<REF>\fR
Show the limits this built\-in rule pack, name@version, sets at the speed. Give it more than once for several packs; rule\-packs lists them.
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
Print the result as JSON
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Show help (\-\-help shows more)
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.SS "opencrs rule-packs"
List the built\-in rule packs, or show one.
.PP
Rule packs encode published regulatory rules, such as a state\*(Aqs roadside signage guidelines, so a screens.json can name them. Without a reference, lists the packs built into this tool with their status. With one, name@version, shows that pack\*(Aqs limits: its creative and playback rules, and where each comes from. \-\-json gives the pack as the rule\-pack document it is, or the list in the shape the WebAssembly module and the language bindings give.
.PP
\fBopencrs rule\-packs\fR [OPTIONS] [REF]
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
Print as JSON
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Show help (\-\-help shows more)
.TP
[\fIREF\fR]
A rule pack, as name@version (default: list them all)
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.SS "opencrs conformance"
Run the conformance suite against a validator.
.PP
Runs the cases in the conformance directory against this tool\*(Aqs own validator, or against another validator given with \-\-cmd. The command is run through the shell with each case directory as its last argument and must print the reports as a JSON array, or for a case with strictest expectations, the strictest requirements of each as a JSON array. Cases for strictest requirements are optional: a validator that doesn\*(Aqt offer them is tested with \-\-no\-strictest.
.PP
\fBopencrs conformance\fR [OPTIONS] [DIR]
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.TP
\fB\-\-level\fR \fI<LEVEL>\fR [default: full]
Level to test: core, layout or full
.TP
\fB\-\-cmd\fR \fI<COMMAND>\fR
External validator to test. It is run with each case directory as its last argument and must print the reports as a JSON array.
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
List passing cases too
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-strictest\fR
Skip the cases for strictest requirements, which a validator may offer but need not. Use it for a validator that doesn\*(Aqt.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Show help (\-\-help shows more)
.TP
[\fIDIR\fR] [default: conformance/0.1]
The conformance directory
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.SS "opencrs completion"
Print shell completions.
.PP
Prints a completion script for the shell to standard output. Save it where your shell looks for completions, or load it from your shell\*(Aqs startup file.
.PP
\fBopencrs completion\fR <SHELL>
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Show help (\-\-help shows more)
.TP
<\fISHELL\fR>
The shell to print completions for
.br

.br
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
bash
.IP \(bu 2
zsh
.IP \(bu 2
fish
.IP \(bu 2
powershell
.RE
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.SS "opencrs version"
Show the tool, spec and rule pack versions.
.PP
\fBopencrs version\fR
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Show help (\-\-help shows more)
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Check a creative against a publisher\*(Aqs screens:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
opencrs check \-\-spec retail.screens.json creative.json
.fi
.RE
.PP
Check against two publishers and save the reports as JSON:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
opencrs check \-s retail.screens.json \-s airports.screens.json \\
    \-\-json creative.json > reports.json
.fi
.RE
.PP
Check a screens.json and a creative.json against the schemas:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
opencrs schema screens.json creative.json
.fi
.RE
.PP
Show what a screen requires:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
opencrs resolve retail.screens.json harbourline.example/retail/screen/HC\-L1\-002
.fi
.RE
.PP
Find a publisher\*(Aqs specs and save the ones to trust:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
opencrs fetch \-\-output specs harbourline.example
.fi
.RE
.PP
Check how a publisher serves its specs:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
opencrs publisher harbourline.example
.fi
.RE
.PP
See what the validator reads from a creative file:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
opencrs probe lunch\-portrait.png
.fi
.RE
.PP
Work out the smallest text readable from 2 to 6 metres:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
opencrs legibility \-\-near 2 \-\-far 6 \-\-screen\-width\-mm 940 \-\-screen\-width\-px 1080
.fi
.RE
.PP
Work out what a driver at 80 km/h can read, and a rule pack\*(Aqs limits:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
opencrs dwell \-\-speed 80 \-\-near 20 \-\-far 150 \\
    \-\-pack au\-nsw\-transport\-corridor@2017\-11
.fi
.RE
.PP
List the built\-in rule packs, then show one:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
opencrs rule\-packs
opencrs rule\-packs au\-nsw\-transport\-corridor@2017\-11
.fi
.RE
.PP
Install the zsh completions:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
opencrs completion zsh > ~/.zfunc/_opencrs
.fi
.RE
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.PP
All commands exit 0 on success, 1 when what they checked failed, and 2 for a usage error or a file that could not be read. Asking for help or the version exits 0.
.TP
\fBcheck\fR
0 passed, 1 a file failed, 2 usage or read error
.TP
\fBschema\fR
0 all valid, 1 a document failed, 2 usage or read error
.TP
\fBresolve\fR
0 resolved, 1 reference not found, 2 usage or read error
.TP
\fBfetch\fR
0 all trusted, 1 a document was not trusted, 2 usage or fetch error
.TP
\fBpublisher\fR
0 no errors, 1 the check found errors, 2 usage error or the index couldn\*(Aqt be fetched
.TP
\fBprobe\fR
0 every file read, 2 usage or read error
.TP
\fBlegibility, dwell\fR
0 worked out, 2 usage error (inputs that make no sense)
.TP
\fBrule\-packs\fR
0 shown, 1 no such rule pack, 2 usage error
.TP
\fBconformance\fR
0 conforms, 1 a case failed, 2 usage or read error
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
\fBNO_COLOR\fR
When set to a non\-empty value, help and error messages are never coloured. They are also plain when output is not a terminal, or when TERM is dumb.
.TP
\fBCLICOLOR_FORCE\fR
When set to a value other than 0, colour is used even when output is not a terminal (NO_COLOR still wins).
.TP
\fBHTTP_PROXY, HTTPS_PROXY\fR
Ignored by fetch and publisher. A proxy would resolve host names where the addresses can\*(Aqt be checked, so they always connect directly.
.SH NETWORK
.PP
Only fetch and publisher use the network, and they share one HTTP client. It is built so that an index cannot point it at a service on the machine or its network.
.TP
\fBScheme\fR
Only https:// URLs are fetched. With fetch \-\-allow\-local\-http, http:// is allowed for localhost, 127.0.0.1 and [::1] only, and only for a domain that is itself one of them: a public publisher can\*(Aqt use it to reach localhost.
.TP
\fBAddresses\fR
Each host, redirects included, is resolved once and refused if any of its addresses is loopback, private, link\-local, shared (CGNAT), multicast, broadcast, unspecified, reserved or for documentation. The connection goes to the addresses that were checked. With \-\-allow\-local\-http, loopback is allowed for localhost, 127.0.0.1 and [::1] only; other internal addresses stay refused.
.TP
\fBRedirects\fR
At most four, and only to https://. A redirect to another host is refused for a document that must stay on its publisher\*(Aqs host and for any request that carries a token.
.TP
\fBTime\fR
Each request, redirects included, must finish within 30 seconds.
.TP
\fBSize\fR
A document larger than 50 MiB (52428800 bytes) is refused. One discovery reads at most 512 MiB in total, indexes and documents together.
.TP
\fBPublisher check\fR
publisher fetches one URL at a time and allows each request 2 minutes, redirects included, and the whole check 5. It reads at most 4000 URLs, 50 MiB for one page and 180 MiB in all (counting what comes over the wire and again once decompressed); a URL past a limit, or not fetched by the time the 5 minutes are up, is reported as not fetched.
.TP
\fBIndex entries\fR
An index may list at most 1000 documents, and at most 1000 delegates.
.TP
\fBTokens\fR
A \-\-token is sent only with requests for that publisher\*(Aqs partner documents.
